      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "activity_id": 79326,
            "id": "3709280721",
            "type": "activity-reference"
          }
        ],
        "id": "1100270003",
        "layout": "vertical",
        "purpose": "learnbydoing",
        "type": "group"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Hydroxy compounds of elements with intermediate electronegativities and relatively high oxidation numbers (for example, elements near the diagonal line separating the metals from the nonmetals in the periodic table) are usually amphoteric. This means that the hydroxy compounds act as acids when they react with strong bases and as bases when they react with strong acids. The amphoterism of aluminum hydroxide, which commonly exists as the hydrate Al(H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "(OH)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ", is reflected in its solubility in both strong acids and strong bases. In strong bases, the relatively insoluble hydrated aluminum hydroxide, Al(H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "(OH)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ", is converted into the soluble ion, [Al(H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "(OH)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": "]"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ", by reaction with hydroxide ion:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "xpsgqlhruy9i1ne",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "en4mm3v398h02de",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "1f94rjnd0qllqll",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm{Al}({\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O)_3(\\mathrm{OH})_3(aq)\\;+\\;\\mathrm{OH}^-(aq)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)\\;+\\;\\lbrack\\mathrm{Al}({\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O)_2(\\mathrm{OH})_4\\rbrack^-(aq)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "In this reaction, a proton is transferred from one of the aluminum-bound H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O molecules to a hydroxide ion in solution. The Al(H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "(OH)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": " compound thus acts as an acid under these conditions. Conversely, when dissolved in strong acids, it is converted to the soluble ion [Al(H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "6"
              },
              {
                "text": "]"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "3+"
              },
              {
                "text": " by reaction with hydronium ion:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "1al7exfvbp760t2",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "nitft81tbhljxzs",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "vb9tse06lpbuuz2",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "3\\;{\\mathrm H}_3\\mathrm O^+(aq)\\;+\\;\\mathrm{Al}({\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O)_3(\\mathrm{OH})_3(aq)\\;\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\;\\;\\mathrm{Al}({\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O)_6^{3+}(aq)\\;+\\;3\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "In this case, protons are transferred from hydronium ions in solution to Al(H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "(OH)"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ", and the compound functions as a base."
              }
            ],
            "id": "vrg2nuvxf6yjbmb",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
